A||||17||ezsurf||2.8 H.O. Head Volume||||5||
Z||000000||ezsurf||03-28-2000||04:17 PM||||Does anyone know the cc volume of the high output heads on the 2.8 engine?  I have an 86se.  I'm trying to calculate compression ratio since I just bored and rebuilt the engine.||||reg||5
Z||000001||87PegasusGT||03-28-2000||07:18 PM||||I know the stock Fiero 2.8 is 8.5:1. Go figure.... The 2.5 was 9:1.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ezsurf||03-28-2000||11:17 PM||||I know what the book specs say, but when you bore out the block the compression ratio will change.  The compression ratio will tell you what octane fuel to use, and how hot the spark plug needs to be.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||03-29-2000||02:43 AM||||The Fiero 2.8L compression is 8.9:1.||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||03-29-2000||12:22 PM||||Terry is correct, stock Fiero V6 is 8.9:1 compression ratio. A 0.030" overbore might increase the C/R a couple tenths of a point. According to Mark who owns the machine shop I use, most oversize pistons actually compensate for this in order to keep the ratio the same as original. Of course, if you had the heads milled that will raise it another tenth of a point or two also.||||reg||1
Z||000005||87PegasusGT||03-29-2000||02:42 PM||||My bad... Thats what I meant||||reg||1
Z||000006||fiero56||03-29-2000||03:06 PM||||I think he is asking for the stock cubic volume for each cylinder in the heads.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 03-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||ezsurf||03-29-2000||03:31 PM||||That is exactly what I am looking for.  The cc volume of the combustion chamber in the head.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fiero56||03-29-2000||04:49 PM||||I have never come across that information, and I have read TONS of Fiero stuff. Might be in the factory service manual. There are kits available to buy, that allow you to find the volume. I don't know how much they cost though. Pretty simple, they use a colored liquid(water maybe), a plastic plate that goes over the combustion chamber, and a tube with volumes written on it. Once the combustion chamber is full, you read the cc's off the metered tube.||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||03-29-2000||05:26 PM||||A good machine shop will have combustion chamber volume specs, but unless you've had head work done, boring out the cylinders will not effect the head combustion chamber volume.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 03-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||03-29-2000||05:54 PM||||It's a pretty simple task if you have the heads off (almost impossible with them on :P ).<P>Here is the short version:<P>- Clean the combustion chamber of all coarbon deposits (the cleaner the better)<P>- Take a section of plexi-glass large enough to cover at least one chamber and drill two hole about an inch apart and about 1/4 inch in diameter.<P>- Lay the plexi-glass piece over the combustion chamber using vasiline or grease to make a seal around the chamber to the glass (be carefull not to squeeze any into the chamber, this will make your measurement inaccurate).<P>- Take a turkey baster (better not let your wife see this) and a bottle of alcohol with a little food coloring for tint (or gin, beer, rum, etc).<P>- Draw the liquid up into the baster and fill the chamber through one of the holes (the second is a vent hole).<P>- Keep track of the amount of liquid. When the chamber is full, add the quantity up and you'll have the volume.<P>I like to use alcohol because it evaporates without any worry of future rust from moisture.<P>Of course, you have to have your valves and spark plug installed and it's also a good practice to seal the valve with a little grease or vasiline.<P>It's always a good idea to do all the chambers for complete accuracy. When you find the largest chamber, you can do a little pocket grinding on the smaller ones until they are all the same.<P>Lastly, quietly slip the turkey baster back into the kitchen drawer without anyone seeing you.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Roy||||reg||10
Z||000011||ezsurf||03-29-2000||09:01 PM||||I wish I had checked the head volume when I had the heads off.  I know somebody has done it.  Boring out the cylinders won't change the head volume, but you are now compressing more air.  I used flat top pistons, so unless the construction of the pistons was modified somehow, the compression ratio should have gone up. By switching to 92 octane fuel, I can already tell the engine runs smoother.  I'm going to switch to a 180 degree thermostat and see how it runs on 90 octane. I'll just have to keep a check on the plugs to see if I need a cooler one.||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||03-30-2000||12:44 PM||||My docs show 52cc for the iron heads and 28cc for the AL heads (not used on the Fiero).<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000013||ezsurf||03-30-2000||08:30 PM||||52cc, I'll buy that.  Thanks dude.||||reg||1
Z||000014||FIEROCIOUS||03-31-2000||12:22 AM||||Does anyone know were to get 9.5:1 pistons for the 2.8?<BR>The reason I ask is when I do the heads I want to unshroud the valves a bit and keep the compression up.<BR>Fiero store advertizes 9.5 pistons but are they?<BR>Can anyone get Part numbers from major companies like federal mogul ect.?<BR>Will there be any problems with valve clearence?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||03-31-2000||01:46 AM||||Run stock 8.9's and mill the heads.<P>If heard people say they have 9.5:1 pistons, but when I check into it, they are 8.9:1.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 03-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||pollock||03-31-2000||01:18 PM||||Here's a way that might be easier.<P>fill the combustion chamber with silly putty or play-doh.  trim it flat to the face of the head with a razor blade.  pull it out, wad it up into a ball if you want, and drop it into a vessel of water with a known volume.  the delta V is your combustion chamber volume. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||terryk||03-31-2000||03:15 PM||||That's a good idea! ||||reg||1
